/-
# Finite groups separating cyclic subgroups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 8.73)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 8.73 (J. G. Thompson): a finite group `G`
separates cyclic subgroups if for any cyclic subgroups `A` and `B` there is `g ∈ G` with `A ∩ B^g =
1`. Is it true that `G` separates cyclic subgroups if and only if `G` has no non-trivial cyclic
normal subgroups?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.Algebra.Group.Subgroup.ZPowers.Basic
public import Mathlib.Basic.Finite.Defs

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SeparatesCyclicSubgroupsKourovka8Problem73Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 8.73 (J. G.
Thompson). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700521 (`KOU-8.73`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

Cyclic subgroups are `Subgroup.zpowers a`; `B^g = g⁻¹ B g = ⟨g⁻¹ b g⟩`. The target is the "if and
only if" over all finite groups `G : Type`. The direction "separates ⇒ no non-trivial cyclic normal
subgroup" is elementary (take `A = B = N`), so the refutation is a finite group without non-trivial
cyclic normal subgroups that does not separate cyclic subgroups.
-/

/-- A group `G` separates cyclic subgroups (Kourovka 8.73): for any cyclic subgroups `A = ⟨a⟩` and
`B = ⟨b⟩` there is `g ∈ G` with `A ∩ B^g = 1`, where `B^g = g⁻¹ B g = ⟨g⁻¹ b g⟩`. -/
def SeparatesCyclicSubgroups (G : Type*) [Group G] : Prop :=
  ∀ a b : G, ∃ g : G, Subgroup.zpowers a ⊓ Subgroup.zpowers (g⁻¹ * b * g) = ⊥

/-- Kourovka 8.73 (J. G. Thompson): a finite group separates cyclic subgroups if and only if it has
no non-trivial cyclic normal subgroup. -/
def separatesCyclicIffNoCyclicNormal : Prop :=
  ∀ (G : Type) [Group G] [Finite G],
    SeparatesCyclicSubgroups G ↔ ∀ x : G, (Subgroup.zpowers x).Normal → x = 1

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Archive answers 8.73 "Not always" with Podufalov's group `G` of order
`4·25·121·289·9`, generated inside `∏ Rᵢ⟨φᵢ⟩` (`Rᵢ` elementary abelian of order `pᵢ²` for `pᵢ = 2,
5, 11, 17`, `φᵢ` a regular automorphism of order `3`) by the `Rᵢ`, `φ₂φ₃φ₄` and `φ₁φ₃φ₄⁻¹`: `G` has
no non-trivial cyclic normal subgroup, while a cyclic subgroup of order `2·5·11·17` meets every
conjugate of itself non-trivially. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems
in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved
Problems, Problem 8.73 (J. G. Thompson); resolution: N. D. Podufalov, Abstracts of the 9th All-Union
Group Theory Symposium, Moscow, 1984, 113-114 (Russian), Kourovka Archive, Problem 8.73: for p₁ = 2,
p₂ = 5, p₃ = 11, p₄ = 17 let Rᵢ be elementary abelian of order pᵢ² with a regular automorphism φᵢ of
order 3; in the direct product of the Rᵢ⟨φᵢ⟩ the subgroup G generated by the Rᵢ, φ₂φ₃φ₄ and φ₁φ₃φ₄⁻¹
has no non-trivial cyclic normal subgroups, and every cyclic subgroup of order 2·5·11·17 meets each
of its conjugates non-trivially; Kourovka Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 8.73: Not always
(explicit counterexample of N. D. Podufalov), https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted separatesCyclicIffNoCyclicNormal_refuted : ¬ separatesCyclicIffNoCyclicNormal

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.SeparatesCyclicSubgroupsKourovka8Problem73Wanted
